{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 opposes the panel but ignores the problem's explicit request to identify evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. Its countercondition for an exhaustive checklist is also self-undermining: if the checklist can be exhaustive and purely algorithmic, the 'pattern matching' objection stops applying and the claim of fundamental inferiority collapses for that entire class of verifiable-outcome tasks, yet the target offers no boundary test to separate that regime from the edge-case regime.","counterexample":[{"task":"diagnostic triage with an exhaustive rule set","checklist":"covers all verifiable outcome categories","panel":"non-experts apply it without edge-case gaps","expert":"fatigue-driven single rater","predicted_by_S1":"panel inferior","actual":"panel more reliable"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 defends the panel but conflates 'variance reduction' with reliability for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes. A panel can have a narrow error distribution and still be systematically biased toward the wrong answer; lower variance around a miscalibrated mean is not greater reliability. Its own countercondition (shared systemic bias in the checklist) is acknowledged yet never operationalized: the target gives no within-evaluation evidence that would separate a genuine accuracy gain from a low-variance compliance process that happens to be wrong in the same direction."},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":false}]}